<Doc
  title="Elixir"
  description="The mcpd SDK for Elixir: a GenServer worker, the span tree assembled from the process dictionary, and no dependencies at all because OTP already ships a JSON codec and an HTTP client."
  lede="A GenServer for the worker, the process for the context, and nothing added to your dependency tree."
  og="docs"
>
  <h2>Install</h2>
  <p>
    Add it to <code>mix.exs</code>. It brings nothing with it.
  </p>

  <pre><code class="language-elixir">{`defp deps do
  [{:mcpd, "~> 0.1"}]
end`}</code></pre>

  <p>
    That is the whole dependency list, and it is deliberate. OTP 27 ships a JSON
    codec (<code>:json</code>) and an HTTP client (<code>:inets</code>), so a
    library whose job is to watch your system does not have to become a reason
    your system's build changes. The other five SDKs make the same promise in
    their own languages.
  </p>

  <h2>Construct the client</h2>
  <p>
    Every option falls back to its environment variable, and code wins.
    <code>Mcpd.start_link()</code> with no arguments talks to a local daemon.
  </p>

  <CodeSwitcher class="not-prose my-6" only={['elixir']} region="init" title="connect" />

  <p>
    Put it in your supervision tree and it behaves like any other worker:
  </p>

  <pre><code class="language-elixir">{`children = [
  {Mcpd, project: "support-desk", environment: "production"}
]

Supervisor.start_link(children, strategy: :one_for_one)`}</code></pre>

  <h2>Record a run</h2>
  <p>
    <code>span</code> is a macro, so a run is a block and its value is the
    block's value.
  </p>

  <CodeSwitcher class="not-prose my-6" only={['elixir']} region="trace" title="record a run" />

  <p>
    Macros have to be required before they can be called, which is a rule of the
    language rather than a choice here. <code>use Mcpd</code> does it for you:
  </p>

  <pre><code class="language-elixir">{`defmodule MyAgent do
  use Mcpd

  def answer(mcpd, question) do
    Mcpd.span mcpd, "agent", kind: :agent_run, input: question do
      do_the_work(question)
    end
  end
end`}</code></pre>

  <p>
    If the body is already a function rather than a block, call
    <code>Mcpd.with_span/4</code> instead. It is the same thing without the
    macro.
  </p>

  <h2>The tree assembles itself</h2>
  <p>
    The active span lives in the process dictionary, and that is the right store
    in Elixir specifically rather than a shortcut. A process is the unit of
    concurrency here, so two requests handled at the same time cannot see each
    other's span, and nothing has to be threaded through every function to keep
    the tree together.
  </p>
  <p>
    A child started three calls deep finds its parent on its own:
  </p>

  <pre><code class="language-elixir">{`Mcpd.span mcpd, "agent", kind: :agent_run do
  plan(mcpd)          # opens a child of "agent" without being told
end

defp plan(mcpd) do
  Mcpd.span mcpd, "plan_answer", kind: :model_generation, model: "gpt-4o" do
    call_the_model()
  end
end`}</code></pre>

  <p>
    The consequence worth knowing: a span opened inside <code>Task.async</code>
    starts a new trace, because that is a different process. Pass the trace
    across with <code>Mcpd.traceparent/0</code> and
    <code>Mcpd.continue/2</code>, the same pair you would use across an HTTP
    boundary.
  </p>

  <SignalFlow class="not-prose my-8" />

  <h2>Nothing blocks, and nothing raises</h2>
  <p>
    Recording a span sends a message to the worker and returns. The network
    happens on that worker, so your agent's latency never depends on mcpd being
    fast, or up, or running at all.
  </p>
  <p>
    A raise inside a block is recorded on the span as an error and then
    re-raised unchanged. mcpd's job is to see what happened, never to decide
    what your code does about it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The queue is bounded, and when it fills the oldest signals are dropped. That
    direction is chosen: when the daemon is unreachable, the newest signals
    describe what is happening now, and those are the ones somebody will want
    when it comes back. <code>Mcpd.stats/1</code> answers what the client has
    done: <code>queued</code>, <code>dropped</code>, <code>capacity</code>,
    <code>exported</code>, <code>failed</code> and <code>rejected</code>.
  </p>

  <p>
    <code>log_level:</code>, or <code>MCPD_LOG_LEVEL</code>, sets a floor under
    <code>Mcpd.log/4</code>. The default is <code>debug</code>, which keeps
    everything. A line below the floor is dropped before the record is built,
    so a debug line in a production deployment costs one comparison. The floor
    covers logs only; spans, metrics, scores and feedback are unaffected.
  </p>

  <h2>Keep payloads in the process</h2>
  <p>
    <code>capture_payloads: false</code>, or
    <code>MCPD_CAPTURE_PAYLOADS=false</code>, keeps prompts and completions
    where they are. Spans still go out with their timing, status, usage, model,
    model parameters, correlation and errors; <code>input:</code>,
    <code>output:</code> and a block's captured value are dropped before they
    reach the queue, so nothing sensitive is queued and nothing sensitive leaves
    on a flush. One span can say otherwise for itself with
    <code>capture_payloads: true</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>How the model was called</h2>
  <p>
    <code>model_parameters:</code> records the settings a model call was made
    with: <code>temperature</code>, <code>top_p</code>, <code>top_k</code>,
    <code>max_tokens</code>, <code>seed</code>, <code>stop_sequences</code> and
    <code>has_response_format</code>. Give it a keyword list, or a
    <code>%Mcpd.ModelParameters&#123;&#125;</code> when you already hold one.
  </p>

  <pre><code class="language-elixir">{`Mcpd.span mcpd, "answer",
  kind: :model_generation,
  model_parameters: [temperature: 0.0, seed: 0, max_tokens: 1024] do
  call_the_model()
end`}</code></pre>

  <p>
    They are settings rather than payload, so they survive
    <code>capture_payloads: false</code>, which is the case that matters: they
    are what somebody reproducing the run has to match. A field you leave out is
    absent and one you give is kept whatever it is, so <code>seed: 0</code> is a
    seed. The schema behind <code>has_response_format</code> is not recorded,
    only whether one was in force. It is an option rather than a setter because
    an Elixir span is described by the options it was opened with, the same way
    <code>model:</code>, <code>provider:</code> and <code>usage:</code> already
    are.
  </p>

  <h3>Streamed calls</h3>
  <p>
    Pass the provider's stream to <code>Mcpd.ModelCall.record_stream/4</code>
    and read the stream it returns instead. It takes OpenAI chat-completion
    chunks and Anthropic message events, and every chunk comes through
    unchanged. The span opens when you first read the stream, under the span
    active in your process, and the first chunk with text or a tool call marks
    the time to first token. When the stream ends, raises, or you stop early
    with <code>Enum.take/2</code> or <code>Enum.find/2</code>, the span gets
    what <code>Mcpd.ModelCall.options/2</code> would give for the same call
    unstreamed, the text as its output, and ends. An error from the stream
    marks the span and reaches you unchanged. The text kept is capped at 32,768
    characters, and <code>mcpd.stream.truncated</code> is set when the cap was
    reached.
  </p>

  <CodeSwitcher class="not-prose my-6" only={['elixir']} region="stream" title="a streamed call" />

  <h2>Assert what you recorded</h2>
  <p>
    <code>Mcpd.Testing.start/1</code> starts a client that records into
    memory, linked to your test. Pass <code>testing.client</code> wherever your
    code takes a client, then read back what it recorded:
    <code>spans/2</code> by name or kind, <code>span/2</code> for the one span
    that must exist, <code>trees/1</code> for each run as a tree,
    <code>logs/1</code>, <code>metrics/1</code>, <code>scores/1</code>,
    <code>recorded_feedback/1</code> and <code>reset/1</code>. Each reader
    flushes first. What it holds went through sampling, payload capture and
    <code>redact</code> as it would on the way to a daemon, and it stays on
    when <code>MCPD_ENABLED=false</code> is set for the test run.
  </p>

  <CodeSwitcher class="not-prose my-6" only={['elixir']} region="testing" title="a unit test" />

  <p>
    A sink is where a drained batch goes. The HTTP transport is the default;
    <code>Mcpd.Sink.Recorder</code> keeps every batch in memory, so a test reads
    back the spans, logs, metrics, scores and feedback the instrumentation
    recorded without a daemon, and <code>Mcpd.Sink.Console</code> prints one
    JSON line per signal to stderr. A sink of your own implements
    <code>Mcpd.Sink</code>; one that raises is a failed batch, counted and
    reported, never raised to the caller.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>Mcpd.record/3</code> queues a span that already ended, as given: its
    own ids, parent, start and end, never re-parented, re-timed or re-sampled.
    That is what an adapter for another tracing system or a batch import holds.
    A record whose ids are not OpenTelemetry-shaped is refused and counted, and
    never raises. <code>external_parent_span_id:</code> on any span carries a
    parent from a tracing system that is not mcpd, for correlation only.
  </p>

  <h2>Durable runs</h2>
  <p>
    The same client serves the daemon's durable runs. <code>Mcpd.Workflow</code>
    builds the definition and keeps the step bodies as three-arity functions;
    a <code>Mcpd.StepWorker</code> registers it, long-polls for leases and
    serves each one on a process of its own, and <code>Mcpd.start_run/3</code>,
    <code>Mcpd.watch_run/3</code>, <code>Mcpd.publish_event/4</code> and the
    rest mirror the run surface one for one. A body raises
    <code>Mcpd.StepException</code> with <code>class: :retryable</code> to be
    retried within the step's budget, and records spans under its step through
    <code>Mcpd.StepContext.span/4</code>. Every span the body closes adds its
    usage to the step's, and the last model seen wins, so the daemon prices
    what the trace shows; <code>set_usage/2</code> is the explicit override.
    <code>on_lease</code>, <code>on_step_start</code>,
    <code>on_step_end</code> and <code>on_conflict</code> sit beside
    <code>on_error</code> on the worker, and <code>StepWorker.stats/1</code>
    counts what it has claimed, completed, failed by class and abandoned by
    reason.
  </p>
  <CodeSwitcher class="not-prose my-6" only={['elixir']} example="workflow" region="workflow" title="a workflow" />
  <p>
    What each construct does, the lease protocol and the run API are on
    <a href="/docs/workflows" class="link">Workflows</a>,
    <a href="/docs/workers" class="link">Workers</a> and
    <a href="/docs/runs" class="link">Runs</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Scores and feedback</h2>
  <CodeSwitcher class="not-prose my-6" only={['elixir']} region="score" title="score and feedback" />

  <h2>Reading it back</h2>
  <CodeSwitcher class="not-prose my-6" only={['elixir']} region="query" title="read the run" />

  <h2>Short-lived processes must flush</h2>
  <p>
    A script or a task that exits quickly loses whatever had not been sent.
    <code>Mcpd.shutdown/1</code> flushes and stops the worker, and is safe to
    call twice.
  </p>

